body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Temporal Civil-Date v1 Snapshot 0.1 [# civil-date-section-1]

  Status: immutable compatibility snapshot
  aeonic-temporal-civil-date-specs-v1-snapshot-0.1, frozen 2026-11-15.
  Proposal lifecycle and disabled publication are preserved. Implementation
  conformance and capacity require separate evidence. Existing comparison and
  numerical profiles, defaults and snapshots remain unchanged.

  The [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.1/civil-date-contract.schema.json | civil-date schema]
  defines closed successful-request/result shapes. Calendar validity, canonical
  sets, fixed diagnostics, selection and work rules below additionally apply;
  schema validity alone is not semantic conformance.

  ## 1. Domain, capabilities and profile [# civil-date-section-2]

  The profile is [$ aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-date.v1]. It MUST be selected
  explicitly by operation policy or reusable context; there is no implicit profile.
  Validate both supplied configurations before resolving policy over context.
  Only the effective profile's availability is resolved. No host locale,
  timezone, calendar, database or process-global resolver supplies missing input.

  There are two independently claimable complete capabilities:

  | Capability | Required operations |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilDate.Relations] | [$ equal], [$ compare], [$ relate] |
  | [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilDate.Calendar] | [$ shift], [$ step], [$ materialize] |

  Each capability requires every admitted pairing/unit and all shared transport,
  metadata, admission and failure semantics. There are no per-case semantic
  opt-outs. A full implementation may claim either or both. Availability is
  host-declared and cannot be enabled by an untrusted logical request. Claim
  selection does not imply that unclaimed operations are unavailable in a provider.

  Civil dates are ISO/proleptic Gregorian dates in years 0001–9999. [$ YYYY-],
  [$ YYYY-MM], [$ YYYY-MM-DD] retain their existing AEON meaning and original spelling.
  The year/month/day granularity defines its complete compatible date set; omitted
  fields are unasserted, not filled with January/first day/midnight. There is no
  clock, offset, geographic, timezone, WTC, non-ISO-calendar or instant resolution.
  A year contains its months and dates without becoming equal to any one of them.

  Derived sets are exact nonempty sets of civil dates on the day grid. They do
  not express probability, an uncertain instant or a complete source history.
  A containing-period display cannot replace their restrictions in a chain.
  Errors never create values; applications may explicitly capture them elsewhere.

  ## 2. Reusable operand bundles [# civil-date-section-3]

  All temporal operands use a bundle. The following are JSON transport categories,
  not new AEON literal kinds or document attributes.

  ### Authored or materialized date [# civil-date-section-4]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": { "category": "civilDate", "value": "2024-03" },
    "originGranularity": "month",
    "history": "none-declared"
  }
  ~~~$

  [$ temporal] is required. [$ value] is preserved date text with one of the existing
  forms [$ YYYY-], [$ YYYY-MM], [$ YYYY-MM-DD], intrinsically validated under the ISO
  calendar. No missing fields are filled in as a first day or clock time.

  For a [$ civilDate] input, omitted history means [$ none-declared]; omitted
  [$ originGranularity] is inferred from the current literal's year/month/day shape.
  If the caller supplies those fields, validate and retain them. Declared origin
  can differ from current literal granularity after materialization; it is
  descriptive history, not authority for interpreting the literal.

  ### Exact derived date set [# civil-date-section-5]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": {
      "category": "civilDateSet",
      "ranges": [{ "start": "2024-03-01", "end": "2024-03-29" }]
    },
    "originGranularity": "month",
    "history": "none-declared"
  }
  ~~~$

  Derived bundles require all three top-level fields. [$ ranges] is nonempty.
  Every endpoint is a complete ISO date, inclusive; each start precedes or equals
  its end. Ranges are sorted, disjoint and nonadjacent: overlap or adjacency must
  already have been merged. Noncanonical input is [$ invalid_operand], rather than
  an instruction for the adapter to repair it. Derived output is canonical.

  origin values are [$ day], [$ month], [$ year], [$ undeclared]. This is a
  small declared origin marker, not a source archive, trusted provenance proof
  or current-operation selector. Shifting, stepping and materialization preserve
  it. The current literal's granularity, exact set and explicit operation retain
  their separate meanings. [$ undeclared] allows a caller to transport a derived
  set without inventing an authored granularity.

  history values are [$ none-declared] and [$ clamping-declared]. The first
  means no clamping history was declared; it does not prove none occurred.
  A shift that clamps any constituent date produces [$ clamping-declared].
  Existing declared clamping is never cleared by a successful subsequent shift,
  step, materialization or zero-count operation. A marker does not recover an
  original day or request restoration of a pre-clamp anchor.

  Bundle fields and descriptor fields are closed. A complete operation response
  is not implicitly unwrapped when passed as an operand: pass its [$ value] bundle.
  Unknown metadata cannot supply a profile, change date meaning or register code.

  ## 3. Request shapes and configuration [# civil-date-section-6]

  Requests have exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. Input admits the generic field
  vocabulary [$ left], [$ right], [$ value], [$ context], [$ policy]; known-operation arity
  then narrows it. [$ context], if present, is an object with only optional [$ profile].
  [$ policy] fields depend on the operation below.

  | Operation | Required operand fields | policy fields |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ equal], [$ compare], [$ relate] | [$ left], [$ right] | Optional [$ profile]. |
  | [$ shift] | [$ value] | Required [$ unit], [$ count]; optional [$ profile]. |
  | [$ step] | [$ value] | Required [$ granularity], [$ count]; optional [$ profile]. |
  | [$ materialize] | [$ value] | Required [$ granularity]; optional [$ profile]. |

  Binary operations reject [$ value]; unary operations reject [$ left]/[$ right].
  Operation-specific settings must be explicitly supplied in policy; a context
  cannot implicitly supply a count, shift unit or output granularity. Unknown
  configuration fields are rejected, including in an overridden context.

  count encoding is canonical signed integer text: [$ 0] or an optional
  minus followed by a nonzero digit and further digits. [$ +1], [$ 01], [$ -0], [$ 1.0],
  exponent spellings and native JSON numbers are not this configuration format.
  This chooses an exact portable argument encoding, not new AEON Number syntax.
  Apply a count-text allowance before conversion and compare large magnitudes
  lexically against the bounded domain when possible; no host-float coercion.

  Example shift:

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "operation": "shift",
    "input": {
      "value": { "temporal": { "category": "civilDate", "value": "2024-02" } },
      "policy": {
        "profile": "aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-date.v1",
        "unit": "month",
        "count": "1"
      }
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  ## 4. Operation meanings [# civil-date-section-7]

  [$ equal] returns a symbol descriptor with value [$ equal] or [$ notEqual] from exact
  date-set equality. [$ compare] returns [$ before], [$ after], [$ equal] or [$ indeterminate]
  under the chronology rules below. [$ relate] returns [$ equal], [$ contains], [$ containedBy], [$ overlaps] or
  [$ disjoint] under the set-relation rules below. Wire symbol descriptors store the label without vertical
  delimiters, consistent with existing Aeonic comparison transport; AEON notation
  for the symbol remains e.g. [$ |before|]. No operation returns a Boolean predicate.


  ### Exact relation definitions [# civil-date-section-8]

  Let A and B be the complete precise nonempty date sets. Date-set equality ignores
  representation, origin granularity and clamping history. It does not redefine
  tick identity or choose an operation from source granularity.

  Chronology returns [$ equal] when A = B; otherwise [$ before] when every A date
  precedes every B date; [$ after] for the reverse; [$ indeterminate] otherwise. Identical
  multi-date sets use extent equality; this is not pointwise universal equality.
  Indeterminate is a successful symbolic result, not false or an error. This
  relation is not a total sorting comparator; overlapping/containing sets and
  disjoint interleaved sets need not have a chronological order.

  The five-way relation returns [$ equal] when A = B; [$ contains] when B is a proper
  subset of A; [$ containedBy] for the reverse; [$ overlaps] when they intersect and
  neither contains the other; [$ disjoint] when they do not intersect. Exactly one
  holds for valid nonempty sets. Disjoint does not itself imply before or after.
  No empty-set semantics, filtering or sorting operation is added here.

  [$ shift] is date-wise over the complete precise set. Units are [$ day], [$ month],
  [$ year], [$ eom], [$ eoy]. A multi-unit request computes a direct target and clamps
  once. Month/year preserve the current day or month/day where possible; EOM/EOY
  preserve current distance from the target period's end, clamping at its start
  when necessary. Day is an ISO calendar-day displacement, not elapsed seconds.
  The agreed calendar-piece recipe computes the exact union and current clamping.

  [$ step] requires exactly one complete day, month or year at explicitly selected
  [$ granularity]. Exact coverage controls admission, independently of source
  granularity. Move that period by [$ count] units of its own granularity and return
  the complete target period. This is not an implicit enclosing-period operation;
  EOM/EOY are date-wise shift units, not aliases for period stepping.

  [$ materialize] requires explicit [$ granularity] = [$ day], [$ month] or [$ year] and
  exact coverage of one complete selected tick. Emit the corresponding existing
  AEON date spelling inside a reusable bundle. March 1–29 cannot become month
  [$ 2024-03]; a containing-period display remains an application presentation.

  All successful transformations remain within years 0001–9999. Any out-of-domain
  image fails the whole request. There is no dropped subset or year saturation.
  Zero-count operations retain ordinary operand/policy/coverage/resource checks.

  ## 5. Closed result envelopes [# civil-date-section-9]

  successful shift result for the example above:

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "outcome": "value",
    "value": {
      "temporal": {
        "category": "civilDateSet",
        "ranges": [{ "start": "2024-03-01", "end": "2024-03-29" }]
      },
      "originGranularity": "month",
      "history": "none-declared"
    },
    "metadata": {
      "evaluation": "exact-for-policy",
      "clamping": "not-applied",
      "effectivePolicy": {
        "profile": "aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-date.v1",
        "unit": "month",
        "count": "1"
      }
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  Shift/step return a [$ civilDateSet] bundle; materialization returns a [$ civilDate]
  bundle with origin/history retained. Current [$ clamping] is [$ applied] or
  [$ not-applied]; step/materialize do not introduce clamping. [$ exact-for-policy]
  means exact evaluation of the declared calendar rule, not reversibility.
  Effective policy contains the profile and that operation's supplied unit/count
  or granularity/count, without unrelated settings.

  Relation results have [$ value: {"category":"symbol","value":"..."}]. Their
  metadata has the same evaluation/current-clamping/effective-profile fields,
  plus [$ operandHistory: {"left":"...","right":"..."}]. Reporting both declared
  histories preserves ownership rather than creating a new temporal value from a
  symbol. Relational output cannot be reused as a date operand.

  Diagnostics have exactly [$ outcome: "diagnostic"], [$ reason] and
  [$ context: {"stage": n, "path": pointer-or-null}], with optional scalar [$ message].
  They have no value, partial ranges, operation metadata, automatic null or
  fallback policy. Messages are non-normative. Operation-specific convenience
  composition, expression evaluation and error capture are separate surfaces.

  Exporting only a bare materialized date drops origin/history. Applications must
  handle that deliberately; this binding introduces no AEON annotation or automatic
  document rewrite to preserve metadata that was not exported.

  ## 6. deterministic validation order [# civil-date-section-10]

  The seven-stage organization follows existing operation contracts, with temporal
  preflight/work/output rules made explicit. Stop at the first failed substep.
  Reason names, stage numbers and exact paths below are normative requirements.

  | Stage | checks and precedence |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | 1 | Unicode-scalar text admission and actual logical JSON UTF-8 byte/depth guards before deeper decoding. [$ invalid_operand] for malformed scalar text; [$ resource_limit] for a reached allowance; null path. |
  | 2 | Strict JSON and duplicate keys; closed request/input shapes; required operation/input and generic input vocabulary; operation/capability availability; known-operation arity. [$ invalid_operand] or [$ unsupported_operation]. |
  | 3 | Context shape, policy shape/required settings, configuration values in context-profile/policy-profile/unit-or-granularity/count order; then effective profile availability. [$ invalid_policy], reached count-text [$ resource_limit], or [$ unsupported_profile]. |
  | 4 | Supported/deferred category and bundle/descriptor/history/origin validation; left then right, or unary value. Canonical ranges checked in array order, start then end; intrinsic date validity before range ordering. Input-range allowance checked before iterating range contents. [$ invalid_operand] or reached [$ resource_limit]. |
  | 5 | Deferred domains in operand order: [$ unsupported_domain] at the temporal/category path. A malformed supported right operand at stage 4 precedes an unsupported left operand here. |
  | 6 | Step's exact-period eligibility ([$ incompatible_operand]); shift/step prospective target-domain failure ([$ unrepresentable_result]); then agreed work admission before construction. Exact image construction and incremental output-range checks. All reached work/range failures are [$ resource_limit]. Relations apply their agreed range-count work guard before returning a symbol. |
  | 7 | Materialization's exact representability first ([$ unrepresentable_result]), emitted date-text allowance next ([$ resource_limit]), and successful-envelope byte preflight before rendering/serialization. All other successful responses also pass the envelope byte guard. |

  At stage 4, require an object [$ temporal] and a recognized string category first.
  For [$ civilDateSet], require [$ originGranularity] then [$ history]; reject unknown
  bundle fields; validate history then origin. For [$ civilDate], validate supplied
  metadata in the same order and apply the documented defaults when absent.
  Check descriptor required fields then unknown fields before payload parsing.
  [$ civilDate] checks its conceptual one-range allowance before detailed date text;
  [$ civilDateSet] requires a nonempty array, applies its length allowance before
  element traversal, then validates each range object's required [$ start], [$ end]
  and unknown fields before parsing its endpoints. Deferred bundles validate any
  supplied history/origin; missing origin is undeclared and their descriptor
  payload is otherwise opaque.

  paths are the reached field for operand/configuration failures;
  input-range allowance uses the descriptor path for [$ civilDate] and the [$ ranges]
  path for [$ civilDateSet]. Stage-6 stepping eligibility uses the operand's
  [$ temporal] path. Target-domain, calendar-work, output-range, representability
  and successful-output byte/character failures use null paths. Exact reason/path
  expectations remain normative at this snapshot boundary.

  Normative precedence examples (not implementation verification):

  | Competing failures | first failure |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | Request bytes exceeded and unknown operation | Stage 1 [$ resource_limit], null path. |
  | Operation unavailable in the declared capability and missing unary operand | Stage 2 [$ unsupported_operation], [$ /operation]. |
  | Shift policy missing count and selecting an unknown profile | Stage 3 [$ invalid_policy], [$ /input/policy/count]. |
  | Malformed overridden context profile and valid policy profile | Stage 3 [$ invalid_policy], [$ /input/context/profile]. |
  | Valid unknown effective profile and malformed date | Stage 3 [$ unsupported_profile], selected profile path. |
  | Count text allowance reached and invalid count grammar | Stage 3 [$ resource_limit], [$ /input/policy/count]. |
  | Empty derived range array and zero input-range allowance | Stage 4 [$ invalid_operand], the [$ ranges] path. |
  | Nonempty derived array over its range allowance and invalid first date | Stage 4 [$ resource_limit], the [$ ranges] path. |
  | Deferred left domain and malformed supported right date | Stage 4 [$ invalid_operand], right date payload path. |
  | Two valid deferred operands | Stage 5 [$ unsupported_domain], left category path. |
  | Partial-month step and zero work allowance | Stage 6 [$ incompatible_operand], [$ /input/value/temporal]. |
  | Shift outside 0001–9999 and zero work allowance | Stage 6 [$ unrepresentable_result], null path. |
  | Valid target with calendar-work exceeded and small output-range allowance | Stage 6 work [$ resource_limit] before image construction. |
  | Unrepresentable materialization and zero work allowance | Stage 6 work [$ resource_limit] before stage-7 representability. |
  | Unrepresentable materialization and zero emitted-text allowance, with work admitted | Stage 7 [$ unrepresentable_result], null path. |

  Materialization pays its one-unit work admission at stage 6 before stage 7. A
  zero work allowance can therefore precede an unrepresentable materialization.
  For shift/step, target-domain/eligibility preflight precedes work rejection under
  the calendar-work recipe. Known input metadata/canonical failures precede either. No validation order
  for non-JSON native graphs is claimed beyond fail-closed cycle/resource handling.

  Within a closed object, check listed required fields before unknown fields;
  unknown fields use decoded Unicode-scalar order. Escape JSON Pointer tokens
  normally; use null if a pointer cannot be represented. For an invalid range
  ordering/adjacency report its start field; for a reversed range report its end.
  Current-clamping/history flags never override a malformed operand or config.

  Proposed recognized deferred tags reuse existing operation vocabulary:
  [$ finiteNumber], [$ exactNumber], [$ exactRational], [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol],
  [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity], [$ nan], [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence],
  [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding], [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress], [$ referenceForm],
  [$ temporal], [$ container]. Their payload is opaque at semantic validation; request
  byte/depth admission still covers it. Unknown category names are malformed
  operands. These tags do not require implementing deferred value families merely
  to reject them.

  ## 7. limits and capacity boundaries [# civil-date-section-11]

  Values below are the initial explicit CTS baseline, not verified native defaults or machine capacity.
  Shared semantic tests must supply an explicit complete map; native defaults and
  their independent capacity checks remain separate evidence.

  | setting | Initial | Scope |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ maxRequestBytes] | 16384 | Actual logical JSON UTF-8, excluding outer CTS transport. |
  | [$ maxRequestDepth] | 32 | Per-path logical container depth; root container is one. |
  | [$ maxInputRanges] | 128 | Each operand; an authored date expands conceptually to one range. |
  | [$ maxCountCharacters] | 32 | Supplied count text before integer conversion/semantic domain checks. |
  | [$ maxOutputRanges] | 128 | Canonical successful shift/step output, guarded before append. |
  | [$ maxCalendarWork] | 4096 | Agreed logical work recipe; not actual iterations or elapsed time. |
  | [$ maxOutputCharacters] | 10 | Emitted bare date text on materialization, after representability. |
  | [$ maxResultBytes] | 16384 | Complete successful compact logical JSON envelope, preflighted before serialization. |

  Trusted resource settings accept objects of known nonnegative safe-integer
  limits; null/scalars/arrays/unknown keys are harness configuration errors, not
  operation values. Outer STDIO/harness framing and provider configuration remain
  outside the logical JSON operation budget. Bindings must preserve raw numerical
  tokens in logical JSON and exact trusted numeric settings, even when malformed
  operation input or opaque payload would overflow/underflow host floating point.

  Successful responses contain only bounded ASCII date/enum/profile/count strings
  and bounded ranges; their exact compact envelope size can be preflighted before
  stringifying the whole response. Diagnostic context has its own bounded logical
  input provenance; successful-output limits must not recursively suppress a
  required no-value diagnostic. Native getters/proxies/custom subclasses and
  complete key enumeration remain a separately documented host boundary.

  All allowances are per operation. Applications manage chain budgets. A materialized
  date output limit does not make a narrower derived set inexact or authorize
  rounding; a successful previous bundle remains separately available to its caller.


  ## 8. Normative calendar-work recipe [# civil-date-section-12]

  Let [$ R] be the number of admitted canonical input ranges. For a complete source
  date [$ d], define [$ monthIndex(d) = 12 * (year(d) - 1) + month(d) - 1].
  Endpoints below are inclusive civil dates. All operand/policy/count validation
  still occurs before any zero-count or other compact shortcut.

  | Operation meaning | Required calendar-work count W |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | Date-wise day shift | [$ R] — one source-range piece per range. |
  | Any date-wise zero-count shift | [$ R] — exact coverage reuse after normal admission. |
  | Nonzero date-wise month or EOM shift | Sum [$ monthIndex(end) - monthIndex(start) + 1] over the source ranges. |
  | Nonzero date-wise same-month/day year shift | The same source-month count; its preservation rule is equivalent to a direct [$ 12 * count] same-day month displacement. |
  | Nonzero date-wise EOY shift | Sum [$ year(end) - year(start) + 1] over the source ranges. |
  | Whole-period stepping | [$ 1], after exact one-period admission at the explicit granularity. The count is a direct period displacement. |
  | Exact date materialization | [$ 1], after operand admission; exact representability and output checks remain separate. |
  | Exact equality, chronology or five-way set relation | [$ R_left + R_right], even if a particular implementation could decide the answer earlier. |

  Each source range may begin/end inside a month or year. Count only its nonempty
  intersections with those periods. If two disjoint source ranges intersect the
  same month, each contributes its own piece; no enclosing-range or cross-gap
  coalescing is allowed. Canonical ranges already merge adjacent dates.

  For nonzero year shifts, this recipe uses month pieces rather
  than a faster year-only algorithm. That common decomposition captures leap-day
  holes using the same preservation rule as month shifts. It is an admission
  recipe, not a claim that every conforming implementation needs twelve iterations
  per source year. A future change to these admission rules needs explicit
  contract/version review rather than an implementation-specific optimization.

  ### Calendar-image preflight [# civil-date-section-13]

  The recipe's local preflight order for a calendar shift is:

  1. Admit request size/depth, configuration and operands through the
     deterministic validation stages. Validate nonempty sorted canonical ranges,
     intrinsic dates, counts, metadata and the unit. Input-range limits bound this
     preparation independently of calendar-work W.
  2. Check the prospective target domain without producing the image. Under the year-domain rule,
     any mapped date outside years 0001–9999 rejects the whole operation. For a
     canonical set, the earliest/latest source coordinates and direct displacement
     determine these bounds; do not scan each day to find a year overflow.
  3. Compute W using source endpoint arithmetic and range counts. Stop a bounded
     sum once it exceeds the configured allowance; an exact over-limit total need
     not be materialized. Do not first construct all month/year pieces, mapped
     dates or output ranges just to discover W is too large.
  4. If W exceeds the allowance, return [$ resource_limit] with no result before
     constructing the calendar image. Otherwise process the admitted pieces.
  5. Normalize the image incrementally with an independent output-range guard.
     Preserve clamping/history metadata. Check remaining result/output limits.

  The stage order in section 6 applies: eligibility/domain preflight precedes
  calendar-work rejection in stage 6. The corresponding reasons are
  [$ incompatible_operand] and [$ unrepresentable_result]. Calendar-domain overflow is distinct from within-period day
  clamping and must not return an in-range subset.

  Zero-count reuse still pays W = R and all input/output checks. It cannot bypass
  unsupported-unit, canonical-form, metadata or complete-period admission.

  ### Exact image of a piece [# civil-date-section-14]

  One piece maps to an inclusive interval. These formulas define the reference decomposition and exact ISO calendar image.

  | Unit | Calendar-piece image |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | Day | Shift both source endpoints by the same number of ISO calendar days. |
  | Month / same-day year | Within a source month, move directly to the target month. Map day [$ d] to [$ min(d, targetMonthLength)]. |
  | EOM | Within a source month, let [$ offset = targetMonthLength - sourceMonthLength]. Map day [$ d] to [$ max(1, d + offset)], preserving distance from month-end. |
  | EOY | Within a source year, let [$ offset = targetYearLength - sourceYearLength]. Map ordinal day [$ k] to [$ max(1, k + offset)] in the direct target year. |

  The mappings are nondecreasing inside each piece and their images have no
  internal date gaps. Same-day month/year clamping occurs if the source piece's
  last day exceeds the target month's last day. EOM/EOY clamping occurs if the
  unclamped first coordinate falls before the target period's first day. Endpoint
  checks therefore detect whether any constituent date was clamped.

  Across source pieces/ranges, target intervals are also nondecreasing under each
  single selected shift rule. Stream them in source order, merge overlaps and
  adjacency, and append only when a genuine gap remains. A newly appended gap
  cannot be filled by a later piece, so output-range allowance can be checked
  before each append without collecting the complete unnormalized image.

  This preserves gaps and handles duplicate images created by clamping. It does
  not enumerate every source day or retain an unbounded tree of calculation
  expressions. An optimized implementation must preserve the same exact set,
  clamping/history facts and logical admission verdict.



  ## 9. Full conformance and delivery boundary [# civil-date-section-15]

  The CTS MUST verify complete result metadata, diagnostics, canonical form and
  actual-result chaining, not only endpoint values. Relations-only claims consume
  derived sets/history without requiring a local arithmetic producer. Calendar
  claims cover all five shift units, all three step/materialization granularities,
  signed/zero counts, clamping/history, direct versus chained behavior and every
  logical allowance. Combined claims additionally cover actual calendar-to-relation
  transport. Input operands are not mutated.

  Operation/chain/law results must use exact expected values and symbolic outcomes.
  Required laws include equality reflexivity/symmetry/transitivity, converse
  before/after, strict-before transitivity and five-way relation consistency.
  Indeterminate pairs are not treated as equivalent sort keys. A malformed or
  resource-rejected input is not a counterexample to a law over admitted values.

  The parallel CTS target is aeonic-temporal-civil-date-cts-v1-snapshot-0.1.
  Artifact checks execute no SUT, establish no semantic pass and cannot create a
  stable implementation claim. Native defaults/capacity calibration and independent
  implementations require separate evidence. Mutable development continues at
  separate paths; no source, schema or runner update may silently modify frozen
  artifacts or their recognized meaning.
`
